    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) Local names of animals and breeds are accepted where the animal is clear, for example 'West African Dwarf goat'. (3) 'Fowl' and 'chicken' are accepted for the same bird. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) In a comparison table, each row must compare the SAME feature across the animals before the mark is awarded. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- A - **goat** (1 mark).\n- B - **cow / cattle** (1 mark).\n- C - **pig** (1 mark).\n- D - **fowl (chicken)** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'she-goat', 'nanny goat' for A and 'hen' for D.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- A - **ruminant** (1 mark).\n- C - **monogastric animal** (1 mark).\n- D - **poultry** (accept: poultry, a monogastric bird) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Monogastric' written for D is accepted, but 'ruminant' is not.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) Any ONE of: **B has four legs and D has two**; **B is covered with hair while D has feathers**; **D has a beak, comb and wings**; **B has horns and hooves** (1 mark).\n- (ii) Any TWO of: **both are ruminants (chew the cud)**; **both are four-legged mammals covered with hair**; **both have hooves and horns**; **both feed mainly on grass**; **both give birth to live young and suckle them** (1 mark each).\n  - *Marking note: In (ii) a difference written in place of a similarity earns no mark.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- P - **fowl** (accept: duck, guinea fowl, turkey) (1 mark).\n- Q - **cow** (accept: goat, sheep) (1 mark).\n- R - **goat** (accept: sheep, cow) (1 mark).\n- S - **cattle** (accept: goats, sheep, pigs, poultry) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A wild animal named anywhere in this part earns no mark.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **A useful material obtained from an animal apart from the main product (meat, milk or eggs), such as hide, skin, horn, bone or dung** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **R and S** (1 mark) **because the skin is obtained after the animal is slaughtered for meat and the dung is the animal's waste, neither being the main product** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Only one mark for naming R and S without a reason.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Heap it and allow it to rot (compost it), turning it from time to time, before spreading** (1 mark).\n- Any TWO reasons: **fresh dung scorches the roots of the crop**; **rotting destroys weed seeds and disease germs**; **it removes the smell and the flies**; **the nutrients are then in a form the plant can take up** (1 mark each, maximum 2 marks).\n  - *Marking note: 'Dry it in the sun' alone is not accepted as the treatment.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- Any TWO, each with a use: **hide or skin** - *leather for bags, sandals and belts*; **horns or hooves** - *combs, buttons, ornaments*; **bones** - *bone meal or glue*; **feathers** - *stuffing pillows, decoration* (1 mark each).\n  - *Marking note: A by-product named without a use earns no mark.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- Body covering - **hair / feathers** (1 mark).\n- Number of legs - **four / two** (1 mark).\n- Type of stomach - **four-chambered (ruminant) stomach / simple stomach with a gizzard** (1 mark).\n- Main feed - **grass and crop residues / prepared mash (with grit)** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Both halves of a row must be correct before the mark for that row is given.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **The fowl has no teeth and swallows its food whole** (1 mark).\n- **The grit in the gizzard grinds the food into small pieces** (1 mark).\n- **Cattle chew with their teeth and chew the cud, so they do not need grit** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Accept 'the gizzard uses the stones like teeth' for the second mark.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **The pigs** (1 mark).\n- **A pig has a simple stomach and cannot digest fibre, so it needs bran and other concentrates, which are costly** (1 mark).\n- **Cattl"
