    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) 'Living' is accepted for biotic and 'non-living' for abiotic, but the terms must not be interchanged. (3) A named Ghanaian example is accepted wherever an example is asked for, provided it fits the category. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) In a food chain the arrows must point from the organism eaten to the organism that eats it. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [5 marks]\n\n- A - **the Sun (sunlight)** (1 mark).\n- B - **a water plant** (accept: reed, water weed) (1 mark).\n- C - **a fish** (1 mark).\n- D - **the mud and stones at the bottom of the pond** (accept: soil, the pond bed) (1 mark).\n- E - **a bird** (accept: a wading bird such as a heron or an egret) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A general answer such as 'plant' for B and 'animal' for C or E is accepted at this level.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- Any TWO of **B**, **C** and **E** as biotic (1 mark each).\n- Either **A** or **D** as abiotic (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Letters alone are enough; names need not be repeated.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Water plant to fish to bird** (1 mark).\n- **Arrows drawn in the correct direction, from the eaten to the eater** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A chain written the wrong way round earns the first mark only if the three organisms are correct.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- P - **terrestrial** (accept: land ecosystem) (1 mark).\n- Q - **aquatic** (accept: water ecosystem) (1 mark).\n- R - **arboreal** (accept: tree ecosystem) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Forest' written for R earns no mark; the category wanted is arboreal.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- Terrestrial - any ONE named Ghanaian land area, for example **the northern savanna grassland** (1 mark).\n- Aquatic - any ONE named Ghanaian water body, for example **the Volta Lake** (1 mark).\n- Arboreal - **the life on the branches of a named tall tree, such as a silk-cotton tree** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: An example placed under the wrong category earns no mark.*\n\n**(c)** [4 marks]\n\n- Q - a named water organism, for example **tilapia** (1 mark), with **gills for breathing in water, fins for swimming or a streamlined body** (1 mark).\n- R - a named tree-living organism, for example **a monkey, a squirrel or a bird** (1 mark), with **limbs or a tail for gripping branches, claws for climbing or wings for flying** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The feature must match the organism named; a feature with no organism earns no mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **The fish breathes through gills, which work only in water** (1 mark).\n- **On land the gills dry up, so the fish cannot take in oxygen and it suffocates** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'There is no water' alone earns 1 mark; the gills must be mentioned for the second.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- (i) **A living part of the ecosystem** (1 mark) **plus a correct example** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **A non-living part of the ecosystem** (1 mark) **plus a correct example** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: An example that does not match the definition earns no mark.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- Grass - **producer, makes its own food using sunlight** (1 mark).\n- Grasshopper - **consumer, feeds on plants** (1 mark).\n- Fungus - **decomposer, breaks down dead material and returns nutrients to the soil** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The name of the role alone is enough for the mark.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **The grass makes its own food from sunlight, so food is produced inside the ecosystem** (1 mark).\n- **The grasshopper and other consumers feed on the grass** (1 mark).\n- **The fungus breaks down the dead material and returns the nutrients to the soil for the grass to use again** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The return of nutrients is essential for the third mark.*\n\n### Question 4 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- **The "
