    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) The names of the five systems must be used exactly: land rotation (accept shifting cultivation), crop rotation, mixed cropping, mixed farming and organic farming. Mixed cropping and mixed farming must not be interchanged. (3) A named Ghanaian crop, animal or district is accepted wherever an example is asked for. (4) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (5) In a table, a correct entry earns its mark even if the handwriting is untidy. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- A - **mixed cropping** (1 mark).\n- B - **mixed farming** (1 mark).\n- C - **land rotation** (accept: shifting cultivation) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Mixed farming written for A, or mixed cropping for B, earns no mark; the two must be told apart.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- Any TWO advantages: **income from two sources**; **manure for the crops**; **crop residues as feed**; **land and labour used all year** (1 mark each).\n- Any TWO difficulties: **more capital needed**; **more labour and skill needed**; **animals may stray into the crops**; **animal diseases must be controlled** (1 mark each).\n  - *Marking note: A general answer such as 'it is good' earns no mark.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- **The plots have lost their fertility after cropping** (1 mark).\n- **The bush that grows and decays on them returns nutrients and restores the fertility** (1 mark).\n- **Reason: population growth has reduced the land available, so fallow periods are too short** (accept: land is now scarce or expensive) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'He is lazy' earns no mark.*\n\n### Question 2 (12 marks)\n\n**(a)** [2 marks]\n\n- (i) **Crop rotation** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **Different crops follow one another in a planned order on the same land, and the plan is repeated** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Land rotation' earns no mark; the land does not change.*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **Groundnut is a legume** (1 mark).\n- **Bacteria in the nodules on its roots fix nitrogen from the air** (1 mark).\n- **The soil is therefore enriched for the crop that follows (and the farmer saves on fertilizer)** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'It is a good food' alone earns no mark in this part.*\n\n**(c)** [4 marks]\n\n- Any FOUR of: **soil fertility is maintained**; **pests and diseases are controlled**; **weeds are kept down**; **the soil structure is improved by different rooting depths**; **the resting year lets the land recover**; **a variety of food and income** (1 mark each).\n  - *Marking note: Two answers that both say 'the soil becomes rich' count as one.*\n\n**(d)** [3 marks]\n\n- **A plan naming all four in a sensible order over four years** (2 marks; 1 mark if one item is missing or out of place).\n- **A sound reason, for example the legume follows the heavy feeder to restore nitrogen, or the resting year comes last before the plan is repeated** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Any order is accepted provided the reason given supports it.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- Opoku - **land rotation** (accept: shifting cultivation) (1 mark).\n- Mariama - **crop rotation** (1 mark).\n- Yaw - **mixed cropping** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The names must match the descriptions exactly.*\n\n**(b)** [4 marks]\n\n- Opoku - **the piece of land changes (the crop may stay the same)** (1 mark).\n- Mariama - **the crop changes, the land stays the same** (1 mark).\n- Opoku - **a large area of land is needed** (1 mark).\n- Mariama - **only a small area is needed** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: A row left blank earns no mark; both columns must be completed.*\n\n**(c)** [3 marks]\n\n- Any TWO of: **several foods from one small plot**; **insurance if one crop fails**; **the soil is covered, so less erosion and fewer weeds**; **the legume adds nitrogen** (1 mark each).\n- Any ONE of: **the crops compete for light, water and nutrients**;"
