    "markingScheme": "*General rules: (1) Accept any other correct and relevant answer. (2) The names of planets must be spelt well enough to be recognised and must begin with a capital letter; 'Earth' and 'the earth' are both accepted. (3) 'Oval' is accepted for elliptical. (4) 'Spinning' or 'turning on its axis' is accepted for rotation, and 'going round the Sun' for revolution; the two must not be interchanged. (5) Where a candidate gives more answers than are asked for, mark only the first ones up to the number required. (6) Do not deduct marks for spelling unless the meaning is changed.)*\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- W - **Mercury** (1 mark).\n- X - **Venus** (1 mark).\n- Y - **Earth** (1 mark).\n- Z - **Mars** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The four names must be in the correct order from the Sun; a correct name in the wrong position earns no mark.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- **The inner planets** (accept: the rocky or terrestrial planets) (1 mark).\n- Any ONE of: **they have solid rocky surfaces**; **they are small**; **they are close to the Sun**; **they have few or no moons** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'They are hot' alone is too vague.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Y, the Earth** (1 mark).\n- Any ONE of: **it is neither too hot nor too cold**; **it has liquid water**; **it has air (oxygen) to breathe** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Because people live there' is a restatement and earns no mark for the reason.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Z is farther from the Sun, so its path (orbit) is much longer** (1 mark).\n- **It also travels more slowly than W, so it takes much more time to complete one round** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: The idea of a longer path is essential for the first mark.*\n\n### Question 2 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [3 marks]\n\n- (i) **A very large group (collection) of stars, dust and gas** (1 mark) **held together by gravity** (1 mark).\n- (ii) **The Milky Way** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Many stars' alone earns 1 mark in (i).*\n\n**(b)** [3 marks]\n\n- **The light of the Milky Way is faint** (1 mark).\n- **The bright lights of the town brighten the sky and hide it, while the village is dark** (1 mark).\n- **Suggestion: travel to a dark place outside the town, or switch off the compound lights and observe on a moonless night** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'Too many buildings in Accra' earns no mark.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Planet, solar system, galaxy** (1 mark).\n- **Reason: the planet is one member of the solar system, and the solar system is one small part of the galaxy** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: No mark for the reason if the order is wrong.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- Any TWO of: **a star gives out its own light and heat, a planet reflects light**; **a star is far larger and hotter**; **stars twinkle, planets shine steadily**; **a planet moves round a star** (1 mark each).\n  - *Marking note: 'Stars are far away and planets are near' is too weak on its own.*\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a)** [4 marks]\n\n- P - **the axis (of the planet)** (1 mark).\n- Q - **rotation** (accept: the planet turning or spinning on its own axis) (1 mark).\n- R - **revolution** (accept: the planet moving round the Sun) (1 mark).\n- S - **the orbit** (accept: the elliptical path of the planet) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Rotation written for R, or revolution for Q, earns no mark.*\n\n**(b)** [2 marks]\n\n- Q - **day and night** (1 mark).\n- R - **the year** (accept: the seasons) (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: Day and night written for R earns no mark.*\n\n**(c)** [2 marks]\n\n- **Elliptical (an ellipse)** (1 mark).\n- **The distance between the planet and the Sun changes as the planet moves; it is nearer at some times and farther at others** (1 mark).\n  - *Marking note: 'The planet is always the same distance from the Sun' is wrong and earns no mark.*\n\n**(d)** [2 marks]\n\n- **The gravitational pull of the Sun holds the planet and stops it flying away** (1 mark).\n- **The planet is also moving forward fast along its path, so it keeps going round instead of falling in** (1 m"
